ed  states to receive the death penalty for  conviction of a federal hate crime so  far all appeals to overturn that  sentence have been rejected in 2015 roof  opened fire on a charleston south  carolina church killing nine  african-american people  all right let's bring in bernard and  terry to break this down bernardo i'll  start with you  what factors do the supreme court have  to take into account here what sort of  decision could we end up seeing  so we're going to see a whole bunch of  conflicting issues in this case because  you are guaranteed under the  constitution the right to effective  representation legal representation so  if that is the case the attorneys are  acting in the best interest of the  client in order to give them effective  representation especially if they  believe a defense that they're going  with can mitigate their exposure and  that is what is going on in this case  the question is going to be in the  court's gonna have to decide who should  we believe who should actually make such  a crucial decision the person who  doesn't have a law degree the client but  who has so much to lose or the person  with a law degree and can apply that  effective representation and see how  it's going to affect the rest of the  country this decision  yeah and terry dylan roof was found  competent to stand trial do you think  that was the correct decision  actually i do think it was the correct  decision here he seems perfectly  competent that simply means that you're  able to understand those proceedings and  that you can take part in your defense  and clearly he is taking part in his  defense it's very different from whether  or not you can assert mental illness  meaning you didn't know the difference  between right and wrong here the  competency means he understands these  proceedings one of the things i was  thinking about is if he has so far  stated he has no remorse for these  killings and he has said that why not  just plead not guilty so it's  interesting he understands what's going  on and yet he still has pleaded not  guilty i don't understand why he doesn't  plead guilty rather  yeah we'll all be waiting to see how  scotus rules on this very interesting  and we'll bring it to you  when we come back we head to florida for  the final chapter in the penalty phase  for robert hayes the convicted killer  learns his fate after a jury deliberates  for hours to decide a sentence  [Music]  welcome back to law on crime daily a  convicted serial killer will spend the  rest of his life behind bars as a  florida jury recommends a life sentence  the jury's ruling came late wednesday  night  sparing robert hayes from the death  penalty  mr hayes the jury having found you  guilty of first-degree murder as a count  one in the indictment i judge you guilty  sends you to life imprisonment without  the possibility of parole in count two  adjudicate you guilty sends you to life  imprisonment without the possibility of  parole and count three adjudicate you  guilty impose a life  in prison without the possibility of  parole each sentence shall run  consecutive to one another  last week a jury convicted hayes of  killing three women laquetta gunther  julie green and iwana patton in the  daytona beach area back in 2005 and  2006.  authorities say the three women were sex  workers  ten years later a fourth woman was  killed in west palm beach during that  investigation hayes dna was linked to  the three daytona beach murders  throughout the trial the state's case  leaned on that dna evidence meanwhile  the defense argued hayes dna was found  because he had sexual encounters with  the women before they were killed hayes  is also charged in that fourth murder in  west palm beach he will go to trial for  that case at a later date  all right let's bring in bernard and  terry bernard i'm going to start with  you hayes avoided the death penalty here  but he's set to stand trial for a fourth  murder could a second jury be as  forgiving  